1	\section{Experiments}
2	\label{sec:experiments}
3	
4	\subsection{Experimental Setup}
5	\label{sec:setup}
6	
7	We apply all three analyses (\S\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}) to the $3 \times 4$ architecture-suite matrix described in \S\ref{sec:arch_data} (Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}).
8	Of the 12 cells, 10 are filled; NORA Object and Long-Horizon lack available checkpoints.
9	For OpenVLA LIBERO-Goal, $N = 63{,}228$ observations; other cells are at comparable scale.
10	For trained models that produce action outputs, counterfactual sensitivity is measured via Cohen's $d$ on action MSE.
11	For reference VLMs (Phi-3-Vision, Qwen2.5-VL) that do not produce actions, sensitivity is measured via cosine distance on hidden representations; these metrics operate on different scales and are not directly comparable (\S\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}).
12	OpenVLA's OXE-pretrained checkpoint produces actions, permitting same-metric comparison (Figure~\ref{fig:result_matrix}; Bonferroni correction in Appendix~\ref{sec:bonferroni_correction}).
13	
14	\begin{figure}[t]
15	\centering
16	\includegraphics[width=0.82\textwidth]{figures/fig_matrix_heatmap.pdf}
17	\vspace{-6pt}
18	\caption{\textbf{Cross-architecture evaluation matrix.} Each cell reports $\Delta R^2$ (peak$-$exit). OpenVLA: VLA-to-VLA; TracVLA and NORA: VLM-to-VLA. Gray cells: checkpoints not released by original authors. Red borders indicate $\Delta R^2 > 0.10$.}
19	\label{fig:result_matrix}
20	\vspace{-10pt}
21	\end{figure}
22	
23	%------------------------------------------------------------------
24	\subsection{OpenVLA: Motivating Single-Architecture Analysis}
25	\label{sec:openvla_routing}
26	
27	Before testing cross-architectural generality, we establish three core phenomena on OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024_openvla} (full analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:openvla_deepdive_appendix}).
28	At L1, three conditions form a gradient: random-weight initialization allocates 0/32 instruction-dominant heads, Llama-2 base~\cite{touvron2023_llama2} allocates 9/32, and VLA-trained allocates 24/32, indicating that VLA fine-tuning shapes instruction routing beyond language-model priors.
29	Counterfactual perturbation produces graded action divergence ordered by semantic distance ($d = 0.81$--$1.18$), and dose-response ablation of instruction-dominant heads yields monotonic trajectory divergence ($d = 0.48$--$0.64$; Appendix~\ref{sec:action_trajectory}).
30	$R^2$ probing localizes training effects at the readout token ($+0.069$ vs.\ $+0.015$ at image\_mean), and readout instruction classification remains at $100\%$ through L31 in the trained model (reference: $82\%$ decay).
31	These phenomena motivate the cross-architecture tests that follow.
32	
33	%------------------------------------------------------------------
34	\vspace{-2pt}
35	\subsection{Exit Collapse: Layer-Wise Action Probing}
36	\label{sec:r2_collapse}
37	
38	We define $R^2$ exit collapse as $\Delta R^2 = R^2_{\text{peak}} - R^2_{\text{exit}}$, the drop in action-probe decodability from peak layer to exit layer (Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}).
39	VLM-to-VLA fine-tuning is associated with substantial exit collapse across all tested conditions: TracVLA~\cite{zheng2024tracevla} $\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.362$ across all four suites, and NORA~\cite{hung2025nora} $\Delta R^2 = 0.308$ (Spatial) and $0.455$ (Goal) (Figure~\ref{fig:r2_collapse}), all exceeding the $\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ threshold for substantive collapse (\S\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}).
40	Under VLA-to-VLA conditions, OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024_openvla} shows only small fluctuations across all four suites ($\Delta R^2 = 0.033$--$0.086$, all below $0.10$); reference baselines similarly show $\Delta R^2 \leq 0.097$.
41	The gap between the largest non-collapse value ($0.097$) and smallest collapse value ($0.156$) spans $0.059$; any threshold in this interval yields identical grouping, and a permutation test rejects random label assignment ($p = 0.008$; Appendix~\ref{sec:delta_r2_threshold}).
42	Even excluding NORA-Goal (whose bootstrap CI is unreliable; Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}, footnote $b$), the VLM-to-VLA range remains $\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.362$ (TracVLA only), consistently exceeding the $0.10$ threshold.
43	Monte Carlo cross-validation supports this separation: VLM-to-VLA trained CIs lie entirely above zero (lower bounds $\geq 0.09$), while VLA-to-VLA trained CIs span or nearly span zero (Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}).
44	
45	\begin{figure}[t]
46	\centering
47	\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_r2_collapse.pdf}
48	\vspace{-6pt}
49	\caption{\textbf{Layer-wise action-probe $R^2$ (LIBERO-Goal, readout token).} Solid: trained; dashed: reference. VLM-to-VLA transitions show pronounced peak-to-exit drops ($\Delta R^2 = 0.16$--$0.46$); VLA-to-VLA remains flat ($< 0.09$). Quantitative $\Delta R^2$ values use demo-level splitting (\S\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}); see Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix} for all cells.}
50	\label{fig:r2_collapse}
51	\vspace{-8pt}
52	\end{figure}
53	
54	\paragraph{NLP finetuning control.}
55	To test whether exit collapse is specific to action-oriented training, we probe three chat-finetuned models that share backbones with our VLAs but lack action heads or robot data: Llama-2-7b-chat (OpenVLA), Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct (TracVLA), and Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct (NORA).\footnote{Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct shares the Qwen2.5 language backbone with NORA's Qwen2.5-VL-3B but lacks the vision encoder and cross-attention modules; see Appendix~\ref{sec:nlp_control_appendix}.}
56	All three produce flat $R^2$ profiles ($\Delta R^2 = 0.000$; $R^2 = 0.155 \pm 0.001$, $0.155 \pm 0.000$, and $0.155 \pm 0.000$ respectively; Appendix~\ref{sec:nlp_control_appendix}, Figure~\ref{fig:nlp_control_r2}), contrasting with VLM-to-VLA collapse ($\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.455$).
57	The absence of exit collapse in any NLP control is inconsistent with exit collapse being a generic consequence of language-model fine-tuning.
58	\textbf{In sum, exit collapse is consistently associated with VLM-to-VLA transitions and absent in both VLA-to-VLA and NLP-only fine-tuning.}
59	
60	\begin{table}[t]
61	\centering
62	\caption{\textbf{Peak-to-exit action-probe drop across the architecture-suite matrix.} $\Delta R^2$ is computed as the difference between the maximum layer-wise action-probe $R^2$ and the exit-layer $R^2$. Values above 0.10 are treated as substantial descriptive drops. OpenVLA is a VLA-to-VLA comparison, whereas TracVLA and NORA are VLM-to-VLA comparisons.}
63	\label{tab:cross_arch_matrix}
64	\small
65	\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
66	\begin{tabular}{@{}lll cccc@{}}
67	\toprule
68	& & & \multicolumn{4}{c}{$\Delta R^2$ (peak $-$ exit)} \\
69	\cmidrule(l){4-7}
70	Architecture & Suite & Transition & Trained & 95\% CI & Reference & 95\% CI \\
71	\midrule
72	OpenVLA & Goal    & VLA$\to$VLA & 0.086 & [$-$0.060, 0.110] & 0.056 & [0.010, 0.080] \\
73	OpenVLA & Spatial & VLA$\to$VLA & 0.037 & [0.010, 0.100]    & 0.044 & [0.030, 0.110] \\
74	OpenVLA & Object  & VLA$\to$VLA & 0.037 & [0.000, 0.090]    & 0.051 & [0.000, 0.090] \\
75	OpenVLA & Long    & VLA$\to$VLA & 0.033 & [0.000, 0.100]    & 0.035 & [0.030, 0.100] \\
76	\midrule
77	TracVLA & Goal    & VLM$\to$VLA & 0.333 & [0.210, 0.450]    & 0.065 & [$-$0.110, 0.050] \\
78	TracVLA & Spatial & VLM$\to$VLA & 0.156 & [0.090, 0.220]    & 0.048 & ---$^{a}$ \\
79	TracVLA & Object  & VLM$\to$VLA & 0.362 & [0.280, 0.500]    & 0.009 & [$-$0.070, 0.020] \\
80	TracVLA & Long    & VLM$\to$VLA & 0.358 & [0.170, 0.530]    & 0.097 & [$-$0.020, 0.110] \\
81	\midrule
82	NORA    & Goal    & VLM$\to$VLA & 0.455$^{b}$ & ---$^{b}$  & 0.081 & [$-$0.010, 0.110] \\
83	NORA    & Spatial & VLM$\to$VLA & 0.308 & [0.300, 0.510]    & 0.089 & [$-$0.010, 0.090] \\
84	\bottomrule
85	\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize NORA Object and Long-Horizon checkpoints are unavailable.}\\
86	\multicolumn{7}{l}{\footnotesize $^{a}$CI artifact from flat $R^2$ profile. $^{b}$Bootstrap CI unreliable (exit-layer $R^2 \approx -0.006$).}\\
87	\end{tabular}
88	\end{table}
89	
90	%------------------------------------------------------------------
91	\vspace{-2pt}
92	\subsection{Instruction Sensitivity: Counterfactual Perturbation}
93	\label{sec:cf_sensitivity}
94	
95	\subsubsection{Instruction Sensitivity in Action-Producing VLAs}
96	\label{sec:cf_action}
97	
98	We first evaluate counterfactual sensitivity in action-producing models using a shared action-space metric.
99	Across OpenVLA and TracVLA, instruction perturbations produce medium-to-large action divergence in all eight architecture-suite cells (Figure~\ref{fig:counterfactual_bar}).
100	
101	\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.46\columnwidth}
102	\vspace{-8pt}
103	\centering
104	\includegraphics[width=0.44\columnwidth]{figures/fig_counterfactual_bar.pdf}
105	\vspace{-4pt}
106	\caption{Counterfactual sensitivity across the architecture-suite matrix. (a)~Cohen's $d$ for four perturbation conditions (E=empty, W=wrong, S=shuffled, P=paraphrased); asterisks mark the top condition per cell; dashed line: large-effect threshold ($d = 0.8$). (b)~Cosine distance on hidden representations (different scale).}
107	\label{fig:counterfactual_bar}
108	\vspace{-8pt}
109	\end{wrapfigure}
110	Every perturbation condition yields $d = 0.45$--$1.52$ (Table~\ref{tab:cf_summary}; full per-condition rankings in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:counterfactual}).
111	The ``empty'' condition ranks first in 6 of 8 cells ($d = 1.17$--$1.52$); ``paraphrased'' ranks last in 7 of 8, indicating that VLAs encode instruction content beyond token-level matching.
112	TracVLA produces systematically higher effect sizes ($d = 0.97$--$1.52$) than OpenVLA ($0.45$--$1.21$).
113	Removing the instruction entirely often produces larger divergence than replacing it with a wrong task, consistent with instruction removal producing larger disruption than instruction substitution.
114	
115	Two OpenVLA cells permit same-metric trained/reference comparison: on Spatial, training increases sensitivity (trained$-$reference difference: $+0.05$ to $+0.24$ across conditions); on Long-Horizon, the OXE-pretrained model exceeds the LIBERO-trained model ($d = 1.05$ vs.\ $0.57$), consistent with the general-purpose model maintaining broader instruction responsiveness.
116	\textbf{Instruction perturbation produces graded action divergence across all tested conditions, with effect sizes reflecting semantic distance.}
117	
118	\begin{table}[t]
119	\centering
120	\caption{\textbf{Counterfactual sensitivity summary.} Cohen's $d$ range and most sensitive perturbation for action-producing models (OpenVLA, TracVLA). NORA uses cosine distance on hidden representations (different scale; see \S\ref{sec:cf_repr}).}
121	\label{tab:cf_summary}
122	\small
123	\begin{tabular}{@{}llcl@{}}
124	\toprule
125	Architecture & $d$ range & Most sensitive & Metric \\
126	\midrule
127	OpenVLA & 0.45--1.21 & empty (2/4 suites) & Cohen's $d$ \\
128	TracVLA & 0.97--1.52 & empty (4/4 suites) & Cohen's $d$ \\
129	\midrule
130	NORA    & 0.28--sat. & --- & cosine dist.$^{a}$ \\
131	\bottomrule
132	\multicolumn{4}{l}{\footnotesize $^{a}$Representation-space only; not comparable to Cohen's $d$ (\S\ref{sec:cf_repr}).}\\
133	\multicolumn{4}{l}{\footnotesize Empty ranks first in 6/8 suites total across both architectures.}\\
134	\end{tabular}
135	\end{table}
136	
137	\subsubsection{Representation-Space Sensitivity in NORA and Reference VLMs}
138	\label{sec:cf_repr}
139	
140	NORA and reference VLMs lack action outputs; their sensitivity is measured via cosine distance on hidden representations (different scale from Cohen's $d$).
141	NORA Goal produces saturated cosine distances ($1.0$); NORA Spatial peaks at $0.275$; reference VLMs show weak sensitivity ($\leq 0.19$; Appendix~\ref{sec:cf_details_appendix}).
142	The trained-reference contrast is consistent with action-space findings, though the different metric prevents direct magnitude comparison.
143	
144	%------------------------------------------------------------------
145	\vspace{-2pt}
146	\subsection{Inherited Routing: L1 Attention Profiling}
147	\label{sec:l1_priors}
148	
149	L1 instruction-dominant head counts across architectures reveal a pattern that contrasts with the training-associated effects documented in \S\ref{sec:r2_collapse} and \S\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}.
150	The TracVLA reference (Phi-3-Vision) allocates 32/32 heads to instruction-dominant attention across all four suites, saturating the capacity of its 32-head L1 before any VLA training.
151	Training slightly reduces this count to 27--30/32, indicating that VLA fine-tuning does not increase L1 instruction-dominant attention in this architecture.
152	The NORA reference (Qwen2.5-VL) allocates 13--14 of its 16 L1 heads to instruction dominance (81--88\%), and training leaves this essentially unchanged at 12--13/16.
153	OpenVLA shows 24--26/32 instruction-dominant heads in the OXE-pretrained baseline; trained values are similar (14--24/32), with the largest change in Long-Horizon where training reduces the count from 25 to 14 heads (Figure~\ref{fig:cross_arch_l1}).
154	
155	If VLA training drove L1 instruction routing, reference models should show low counts that increase after training, as in OpenVLA's random-weight condition (0/32; \S\ref{sec:openvla_routing}); instead, both reference VLMs already saturate or near-saturate, and training leaves counts unchanged or slightly reduced.
156	\textbf{L1 instruction routing is an inherited backbone property, not a consequence of VLA training.}
157	
158	\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.46\columnwidth}
159	\vspace{-8pt}
160	\centering
161	\includegraphics[width=0.44\columnwidth]{figures/fig_l1_attention.pdf}
162	\vspace{-4pt}
163	\caption{L1 instruction-dominant head counts (trained vs.\ reference; heads with $>50\%$ attention on instruction tokens). Reference VLMs already saturate or near-saturate; training does not increase counts. Medians across available LIBERO suites per architecture.}
164	\label{fig:cross_arch_l1}
165	\vspace{-8pt}
166	\end{wrapfigure}
167	
168	%------------------------------------------------------------------
169	\vspace{-2pt}
170	\subsection{Synthesis: Training-Associated vs.\ Inherited Routing}
171	\label{sec:routing_synthesis}
172	
173	The three analyses separate two empirical patterns: exit collapse and counterfactual sensitivity are training-associated (pronounced in VLM-to-VLA, small in VLA-to-VLA), while L1 instruction attention is inherited from reference backbones.
174	Despite qualitatively different reference starting points (OpenVLA: peak at L0 with monotonic decline; Phi-3-Vision: gradual decline from L6), trained OpenVLA and TracVLA converge to nearly identical mid-layer peaks ($R^2 = 0.637$ at L9 vs.\ $0.634$ at L11, $\Delta = 0.003$; Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_arch_r2}, Table~\ref{tab:arch_overview}), suggesting that action fine-tuning drives representations toward a shared mid-layer structure regardless of starting point.
175	These probes measure decodability, not causal reliance (label-permutation controls confirm genuine structure; Appendix~\ref{sec:label_permutation}); matched-size ablation reveals distributed rather than head-specific causal contribution (Appendix~\ref{sec:mechanistic_interpretation}).
176	With this caveat, the consistent three-way pattern provides a first cross-architecture decomposition of VLA instruction routing.
177

