\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We apply all three analyses (\S\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}) to the $3 \times 4$ architecture-suite matrix described in \S\ref{sec:arch_data} (Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}).
Of the 12 cells, 10 are filled; NORA Object and Long-Horizon lack available checkpoints.
For OpenVLA LIBERO-Goal, $N = 63{,}228$ observations; other cells are at comparable scale.
For trained models that produce action outputs, counterfactual sensitivity is measured via Cohen's $d$ on action MSE.
For reference VLMs (Phi-3-Vision, Qwen2.5-VL) that do not produce actions, sensitivity is measured via cosine distance on hidden representations; these metrics operate on different scales and are not directly comparable (\S\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}).
OpenVLA's OXE-pretrained checkpoint produces actions, permitting same-metric comparison (Figure~\ref{fig:result_matrix}; Bonferroni correction in Appendix~\ref{sec:bonferroni_correction}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.82\textwidth]{figures/fig_matrix_heatmap.pdf}
\vspace{-6pt}
\caption{\textbf{Cross-architecture evaluation matrix.} Each cell reports $\Delta R^2$ (peak$-$exit). OpenVLA: VLA-to-VLA; TracVLA and NORA: VLM-to-VLA. Gray cells: checkpoints not released by original authors. Red borders indicate $\Delta R^2 > 0.10$.}
\label{fig:result_matrix}
\vspace{-10pt}
\end{figure}

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{OpenVLA: Motivating Single-Architecture Analysis}
\label{sec:openvla_routing}

Before testing cross-architectural generality, we establish three core phenomena on OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024_openvla} (full analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:openvla_deepdive_appendix}).
At L1, three conditions form a gradient: random-weight initialization allocates 0/32 instruction-dominant heads, Llama-2 base~\cite{touvron2023_llama2} allocates 9/32, and VLA-trained allocates 24/32, indicating that VLA fine-tuning shapes instruction routing beyond language-model priors.
Counterfactual perturbation produces graded action divergence ordered by semantic distance ($d = 0.81$--$1.18$), and dose-response ablation of instruction-dominant heads yields monotonic trajectory divergence ($d = 0.48$--$0.64$; Appendix~\ref{sec:action_trajectory}).
$R^2$ probing localizes training effects at the readout token ($+0.069$ vs.\ $+0.015$ at image\_mean), and readout instruction classification remains at $100\%$ through L31 in the trained model (reference: $82\%$ decay).
These phenomena motivate the cross-architecture tests that follow.

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\vspace{-2pt}
\subsection{Exit Collapse: Layer-Wise Action Probing}
\label{sec:r2_collapse}

We define $R^2$ exit collapse as $\Delta R^2 = R^2_{\text{peak}} - R^2_{\text{exit}}$, the drop in action-probe decodability from peak layer to exit layer (Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}).
VLM-to-VLA fine-tuning is associated with substantial exit collapse across all tested conditions: TracVLA~\cite{zheng2024tracevla} $\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.362$ across all four suites, and NORA~\cite{hung2025nora} $\Delta R^2 = 0.308$ (Spatial) and $0.455$ (Goal) (Figure~\ref{fig:r2_collapse}), all exceeding the $\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ threshold for substantive collapse (\S\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}).
Under VLA-to-VLA conditions, OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024_openvla} shows only small fluctuations across all four suites ($\Delta R^2 = 0.033$--$0.086$, all below $0.10$); reference baselines similarly show $\Delta R^2 \leq 0.097$.
The gap between the largest non-collapse value ($0.097$) and smallest collapse value ($0.156$) spans $0.059$; any threshold in this interval yields identical grouping, and a permutation test rejects random label assignment ($p = 0.008$; Appendix~\ref{sec:delta_r2_threshold}).
Even excluding NORA-Goal (whose bootstrap CI is unreliable; Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}, footnote $b$), the VLM-to-VLA range remains $\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.362$ (TracVLA only), consistently exceeding the $0.10$ threshold.
Monte Carlo cross-validation supports this separation: VLM-to-VLA trained CIs lie entirely above zero (lower bounds $\geq 0.09$), while VLA-to-VLA trained CIs span or nearly span zero (Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_r2_collapse.pdf}
\vspace{-6pt}
\caption{\textbf{Layer-wise action-probe $R^2$ (LIBERO-Goal, readout token).} Solid: trained; dashed: reference. VLM-to-VLA transitions show pronounced peak-to-exit drops ($\Delta R^2 = 0.16$--$0.46$); VLA-to-VLA remains flat ($< 0.09$). Quantitative $\Delta R^2$ values use demo-level splitting (\S\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}); see Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix} for all cells.}
\label{fig:r2_collapse}
\vspace{-8pt}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{NLP finetuning control.}
To test whether exit collapse is specific to action-oriented training, we probe three chat-finetuned models that share backbones with our VLAs but lack action heads or robot data: Llama-2-7b-chat (OpenVLA), Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct (TracVLA), and Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct (NORA).\footnote{Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct shares the Qwen2.5 language backbone with NORA's Qwen2.5-VL-3B but lacks the vision encoder and cross-attention modules; see Appendix~\ref{sec:nlp_control_appendix}.}
All three produce flat $R^2$ profiles ($\Delta R^2 = 0.000$; $R^2 = 0.155 \pm 0.001$, $0.155 \pm 0.000$, and $0.155 \pm 0.000$ respectively; Appendix~\ref{sec:nlp_control_appendix}, Figure~\ref{fig:nlp_control_r2}), contrasting with VLM-to-VLA collapse ($\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.455$).
The absence of exit collapse in any NLP control is inconsistent with exit collapse being a generic consequence of language-model fine-tuning.
\textbf{In sum, exit collapse is consistently associated with VLM-to-VLA transitions and absent in both VLA-to-VLA and NLP-only fine-tuning.}

