\end{table}

\subsubsection{Representation-Space Sensitivity in NORA and Reference VLMs}
\label{sec:cf_repr}

NORA and reference VLMs lack action outputs; their sensitivity is measured via cosine distance on hidden representations (different scale from Cohen's $d$).
NORA Goal produces saturated cosine distances ($1.0$); NORA Spatial peaks at $0.275$; reference VLMs show weak sensitivity ($\leq 0.19$; Appendix~\ref{sec:cf_details_appendix}).
The trained-reference contrast is consistent with action-space findings, though the different metric prevents direct magnitude comparison.

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\vspace{-2pt}
\subsection{Inherited Routing: L1 Attention Profiling}
\label{sec:l1_priors}

L1 instruction-dominant head counts across architectures reveal a pattern that contrasts with the training-associated effects documented in \S\ref{sec:r2_collapse} and \S\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}.
The TracVLA reference (Phi-3-Vision) allocates 32/32 heads to instruction-dominant attention across all four suites, saturating the capacity of its 32-head L1 before any VLA training.
Training slightly reduces this count to 27--30/32, indicating that VLA fine-tuning does not increase L1 instruction-dominant attention in this architecture.
The NORA reference (Qwen2.5-VL) allocates 13--14 of its 16 L1 heads to instruction dominance (81--88\%), and training leaves this essentially unchanged at 12--13/16.
OpenVLA shows 24--26/32 instruction-dominant heads in the OXE-pretrained baseline; trained values are similar (14--24/32), with the largest change in Long-Horizon where training reduces the count from 25 to 14 heads (Figure~\ref{fig:cross_arch_l1}).

If VLA training drove L1 instruction routing, reference models should show low counts that increase after training, as in OpenVLA's random-weight condition (0/32; \S\ref{sec:openvla_routing}); instead, both reference VLMs already saturate or near-saturate, and training leaves counts unchanged or slightly reduced.
\textbf{L1 instruction routing is an inherited backbone property, not a consequence of VLA training.}

\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.46\columnwidth}
\vspace{-8pt}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.44\columnwidth]{figures/fig_l1_attention.pdf}
\vspace{-4pt}
\caption{L1 instruction-dominant head counts (trained vs.\ reference; heads with $>50\%$ attention on instruction tokens). Reference VLMs already saturate or near-saturate; training does not increase counts. Medians across available LIBERO suites per architecture.}
\label{fig:cross_arch_l1}
\vspace{-8pt}
\end{wrapfigure}

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\vspace{-2pt}
\subsection{Synthesis: Training-Associated vs.\ Inherited Routing}
\label{sec:routing_synthesis}

The three analyses separate two empirical patterns: exit collapse and counterfactual sensitivity are training-associated (pronounced in VLM-to-VLA, small in VLA-to-VLA), while L1 instruction attention is inherited from reference backbones.
Despite qualitatively different reference starting points (OpenVLA: peak at L0 with monotonic decline; Phi-3-Vision: gradual decline from L6), trained OpenVLA and TracVLA converge to nearly identical mid-layer peaks ($R^2 = 0.637$ at L9 vs.\ $0.634$ at L11, $\Delta = 0.003$; Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_arch_r2}, Table~\ref{tab:arch_overview}), suggesting that action fine-tuning drives representations toward a shared mid-layer structure regardless of starting point.
These probes measure decodability, not causal reliance (label-permutation controls confirm genuine structure; Appendix~\ref{sec:label_permutation}); matched-size ablation reveals distributed rather than head-specific causal contribution (Appendix~\ref{sec:mechanistic_interpretation}).
With this caveat, the consistent three-way pattern provides a first cross-architecture decomposition of VLA instruction routing.

\enlargethispage{8\baselineskip}
\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\vspace{-4pt}

\paragraph{Scope and limitations.}\looseness=-1
All findings are correlational (confounded by data distribution and optimization; $N = 2$ VLM-to-VLA transitions; Appendix~\ref{sec:claim_evidence_appendix}); we analyze causal-decoder VLAs on LIBERO only (flow-matching~\cite{black2024_pi0} and diffusion~\cite{ghosh2024_octo} architectures may differ); all analyses are open-loop.
Trained $\Delta R^2$ correlates with published success rates ($\rho = 0.583$, $p = 0.10$; Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}) but is underpowered ($n = 9$; effect size is large, $|\rho| > 0.5$); tracing collapse onset remains for future work.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}\looseness=-1
$\Delta R^2$ provides a cheap diagnostic ($> 0.10$ signals reorganization); preserved VLA-to-VLA exit structure justifies staged pipelines; mid-layer probes outperform exit-layer probes where collapse is pronounced (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}).
For example, a practitioner training a new VLA from a VLM backbone can monitor $\Delta R^2$ during training; values exceeding $0.10$ indicate that action-relevant restructuring has begun, providing a checkpoint-selection criterion that does not require expensive rollout evaluation.

\vspace{-2pt}
\paragraph{Conclusion.}\looseness=-1
Across three architectures and four LIBERO suites, VLM-to-VLA fine-tuning produces exit collapse ($\Delta R^2 = 0.16$--$0.46$) while VLA-to-VLA conditions remain flat ($< 0.10$).
All trained VLAs show graded counterfactual sensitivity (Cohen's $d = 0.45$--$1.52$ for action-producing models; cosine distance for NORA); L1 instruction attention is inherited from pretrained backbones; NLP controls ($\Delta R^2 = 0.000$) confirm transition-type specificity, providing reusable diagnostics for future VLA evaluation and training pipeline design.
Code and data will be released upon acceptance.

